        {"kind": "h2", "text": "5.3 User Flows"},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Boot: MCP client spawns node index.js → dotenv loads .env → loadConfig() validates token, API version and numeric settings, throwing on failure → GraphClient constructed → read tools registered, write tools registered only if allowWrites → stdio transport connected → one-line status written to stderr.",
            "Read call: client sends tools/call → handler validates arguments locally → paginate() issues the first request with limit=META_PAGE_LIMIT → follows paging.next, retrying transient failures → returns {data, pages, truncated} → serialised as pretty JSON text content.",
            "Truncation: page count reaches META_MAX_PAGES while a next cursor remains → paginate returns immediately with truncated=true, and that flag is part of the JSON the agent receives, so the shortfall is visible rather than silent.",
            "Error: non-retryable Graph error, or retry budget exhausted → GraphError propagates to index.js → returned as isError content naming the tool, message, code/subcode and fbtrace_id.",
            "Write (enabled): operator sets META_ALLOW_WRITES=true and restarts → the 4 write tools appear in tools/list → create_* produces PAUSED objects → a human reviews in Ads Manager → update_status ACTIVE is the single deliberate step that begins spending.",
            "Write (default): tools/list contains 8 tools; any create_* call fails as unknown tool, with a hint naming META_ALLOW_WRITES.",
        ]},

        {"kind": "h1", "text": "6. Non-Functional Requirements"},
        {"kind": "table", "header": ["Category", "Requirement"], "rows": [
            ["Performance", "Per-request timeout META_TIMEOUT_MS (default 60,000 ms). Page size META_PAGE_LIMIT (default 200). Latency is dominated by Graph and by page count; get_account_snapshot issues many paginated calls and is expected to be the slowest tool by a wide margin."],
            ["Scalability", "Single-process, single-token, one client per process. Bounded by META_MAX_PAGES (default 200 pages) per edge. No concurrency control beyond what the MCP client imposes; parallel callers share the same per-app and per-ad-account rate budget."],
            ["Availability", "Availability equals Meta Graph availability. Transient failures (codes 1, 2, 4, 17, 32, 341, 613; HTTP 429/500/502/503/504) are retried up to META_MAX_RETRIES (default 4) with jittered exponential backoff. There is no circuit breaker and no fallback data source."],
            ["Security", "META_ACCESS_TOKEN is read from the environment/.env and sent as a Bearer header; it is never logged and is stripped from paging.next URLs before reuse. Boot fails on the placeholder token value. Writes require a deliberate META_ALLOW_WRITES=true. Token scope should be the minimum needed: ads_read for read-only deployments, ads_management only where writes are intended."],
            ["Privacy / Compliance", "Handles client advertising performance and account financial data (balance, spend cap, funding source) belonging to third parties. No end-user personal data is fetched, but demographic breakdowns return aggregate audience segments. No data is persisted by the server; retention is entirely the MCP client's transcript."],
            ["Accessibility", "N/A — no human-facing UI. Operator-facing output is plain text on stdout/stderr and is screen-reader compatible by construction."],
            ["Observability", "One stderr line at startup (Graph version, tool count, write state); errors returned to the caller carry Graph code, subcode and fbtrace_id. There is no structured logging, no metrics, no tracing, and no per-request log. Insufficient for unattended operation — recorded as a risk."],
            ["Localization", "N/A — no user-facing copy to translate. Tool descriptions and errors are English only. Monetary values are returned in each account's own currency and in Meta's minor-unit convention; the server does not convert or format them, so callers must not assume USD."],
        ]},

        {"kind": "h1", "text": "7. Technical Considerations"},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "7.1 Architecture & Dependencies"},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Plain Node ESM JavaScript, no TypeScript and no build step. Engines: node >= 18 (native fetch and AbortController are required). Roughly 695 lines across five files.",
            "index.js — MCP server wiring, tool registry, ListTools/CallTool handlers, error formatting, stdio transport.",
            "src/config.js — environment parsing and fail-fast validation; produces the single Config object.",
            "src/graph.js — GraphClient and GraphError: retry, cursor pagination, error normalisation. Contains no MCP concepts.",
            "src/tools.js — tool definitions with hand-written JSON Schema, argument validation, and handlers; readTools() and writeTools() are separate exports.",
            "src/fields.js — default field sets and enumerations (account, campaign, adset, ad, creative, insights full and lite, insight levels, date presets).",
            "Dependencies: @modelcontextprotocol/sdk ^1.29.0 (the newest SDK version in the monorepo) and dotenv ^17.4.2. No zod; input schemas are hand-written JSON Schema objects, so arguments are validated by hand in the handlers rather than by a schema library.",
            "Scripts: start (node index.js) and check (node --check per file, a syntax pass only). There is no test script and no tests.",
        ]},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "7.2 Data Model & APIs"},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Base URL https://graph.facebook.com/${META_API_VERSION}, default v23.0. Auth is an Authorization: Bearer header.",
            "Read edges: me/adaccounts, act_<id>, act_<id>/campaigns, /adsets, /ads, /adcreatives, and <node>/insights at account, campaign, adset and ad level.",
            "Writes are POSTs with a JSON body to the corresponding creation edges, and to the node itself for update_status.",
            "Pagination contract returned to callers: {data: [...], pages: <int>, truncated: <bool>}. requestAbsolute() re-fetches paging.next by parsing its query string and deleting any access_token it carries, so the configured credential is always the one used.",
            "No local schema, cache or storage. All responses are serialised to pretty-printed JSON in a single text content block, which means very large snapshots produce very large messages.",
        ]},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "7.3 Integrations"},
        {"kind": "table", "header": ["Dependency", "Owner", "Type", "Status"], "rows": [
            ["Meta Marketing / Graph API v23.0", "Meta Platforms", "External REST API", "Live; version pinned by META_API_VERSION"],
            ["Meta system-user access token", "TBD — Business Manager owner", "Credential", "Required; boot fails without it"],
            ["@modelcontextprotocol/sdk ^1.29.0", "Anthropic / MCP", "npm dependency", "Active; newest SDK pin in the monorepo"],
            ["dotenv ^17.4.2", "Open source", "npm dependency", "Active"],
            ["Node.js >= 18 runtime", "Node.js project", "Runtime", "Required for global fetch/AbortController"],
            ["MCP client (Claude Code)", "Anthropic", "Host process", "Registered as meta-ads in ~/.claude.json"],
            ["archive/mcp-meta-ads-v1-deprecated/", "HaorGrix", "Superseded code", "Deprecated; reference only, unmaintained"],
        ]},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "7.4 Constraints & Assumptions"},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Meta access tokens expire: long-lived user tokens last roughly 60 days; system-user tokens do not expire, which is why system-user tokens are the assumed deployment.",
            "Meta rate limits per app and per ad account, with Business Use Case throttling that tightens as spend and call volume rise. The retry policy absorbs throttling but cannot create headroom; get_account_snapshot on a large account is the likeliest trigger.",
            "Meta deprecates Graph API versions on roughly a two-year cycle, hence META_API_VERSION being configurable rather than hard-coded.",
            "META_MAX_PAGES caps results. Very large accounts can therefore be returned incomplete; the code does set truncated=true so the caller can see it, but nothing forces the caller to act on that flag.",
            "Tool names are not namespaced with a server prefix. list_campaigns, list_ads and get_insights are generic enough to collide with another mounted server (the same issue exists in google-search-console-mcp).",
            "Being plain JavaScript, there is no typecheck beyond node --check, which only verifies syntax. JSDoc annotations exist but are not enforced.",
            "Assumes a single Business Manager identity per process; multiple tokens require multiple server instances.",
            "Assumes the caller supplies valid Meta targeting specs for create_adset; the server does not validate targeting semantics.",
        ]},
        {"kind": "h2", "text": "7.5 Analytics & Instrumentation"},
        {"kind": "table", "header": ["Event", "Trigger", "Properties", "Destination"], "rows": [
            ["Startup line", "Successful transport connect", "Graph API version, registered tool count, writes ENABLED/disabled", "stderr"],
            ["Config failure", "Missing/placeholder token, malformed API version, non-positive integer setting", "Error message naming the offending variable", "stderr, process exit"],
            ["Tool error result", "GraphError or unexpected exception in a handler", "Tool name, Graph message, code/subcode, fbtrace_id", "MCP response (isError)"],
            ["Unhandled rejection", "Any unawaited promise rejection", "Reason message", "stderr"],
            ["Pagination outcome", "Every paginated read", "pages, truncated", "MCP response body (data, not telemetry)"],
        ]},
        {"kind": "meta", "text": "There is no telemetry pipeline. Everything above is either console output or part of the tool payload; nothing is aggregated or retained."},

        {"kind": "h1", "text": "8. Design & UX"},
        {"kind": "para", "text": "There is no graphical interface. The server is a headless stdio process whose only consumer is an AI agent, so \"design\" here means the shape of the tool surface the model reasons over."},
        {"kind": "bullets", "items": [
            "Tool naming: verb-first and predictable (list_*, get_*, create_*, update_*), which makes intent guessable from the name alone. The gap is namespacing — no meta_ prefix, so names can collide when several servers are mounted in one client. Adding a prefix is a breaking change and is tracked as an open question.",
            "Descriptions are the agent's only affordance. They carry the load a UI would: get_insights states the supported levels and that it is fully paginated; every write tool states in its own description that it creates PAUSED objects and requires META_ALLOW_WRITES=true; update_status states plainly that setting ACTIVE can start spending.",
            "Error legibility: failures come back as human-readable sentences naming the tool, the Graph message, the numeric code/subcode and the fbtrace_id, so an agent can distinguish a bad argument from a throttle, and an engineer can take the trace id to Meta support.",
            "Argument validation is deliberately pre-flight and specific: an ad_account_id that is not act_<digits> is rejected with the value quoted back, before any network call, which keeps correction cheap.",
            "Guard ergonomics: the write gate is enforced by absence, not refusal. When writes are off the tools are never registered, so the model never sees an affordance it cannot use and cannot be argued into trying. The cost is that an agent given a stale tool list gets an unknown-tool error rather than a permission error; the message compensates by naming META_ALLOW_WRITES.",
            "Output is pretty-printed JSON in one text block. Readable for a human skimming a transcript, but there is no summarisation, so a full snapshot of a large account is a very large message.",
        ]},

        {"kind": "break"},
